(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Single and Multilevel Inheritance in Python. Detail super\(\) proxy initialization, method resolution, and the Liskov Substitution Principle \(LSP\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Principles of Inheritance:) Tj
T*
(   Inheritance is a fundamental OOP mechanism whereby a derived class \(subclass\) inherits state) Tj
T*
(   and behavior from a base class \(superclass\), fostering code reuse and extensibility:) Tj
T*
(   - Single Inheritance: A derived class inherits from exactly one direct parent class.) Tj
T*
(   - Multilevel Inheritance: A class inherits from a derived class, forming a vertical hierarchy) Tj
T*
(   chain: A -> B -> C.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Role of super\(\):) Tj
T*
(   In a derived class, super\(\).__init__\(*args\) initializes the base class state. Using super\(\)) Tj
T*
(   ensures cooperative method resolution and prevents hardcoding the parent class name.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The Liskov Substitution Principle \(LSP\):) Tj
T*
(   A fundamental SOLID design principle: Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with) Tj
T*
(   objects of a subclass without affecting the correctness of the program.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Python Implementation:) Tj
T*
(   class Vehicle:) Tj
T*
(   """Base superclass in multilevel hierarchy.""") Tj
T*
(   def __init__\(self, vin: str, manufacturer: str\):) Tj
T*
(   self.vin = vin) Tj
T*
(   self.manufacturer = manufacturer) Tj
T*
ET

